course: books
lesson: 37
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter IV, part 1: Mr. Badger (the door in the snow and the fire-lit kitchen)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Rat and Mole stand stamping in the snow for ages until slow, shuffling footsteps come to the door — like someone in slippers too big for him, Mole says, and he is right. A long snout and two sleepy eyes peep out, and a grumpy voice asks who is disturbing people on such a night.
explain: The moment he hears it is Ratty, the Badger's voice changes completely: «Come along in, both of you, at once.» He is in his dressing-gown with a candle, on his way to bed, but he pats their heads like a kind old uncle and leads them down a shabby passage to his kitchen.
explain: The kitchen is the warmest, safest room you can imagine: a big log fire, a red brick floor, high-backed benches, hams and onions hanging from the beams, plates winking on the dresser. Badger gives them dry clothes and slippers, washes Mole's cut leg and puts a plaster on it.
explain: Then comes a huge supper. Nobody talks for a long time, and when they do, they talk with their mouths full and their elbows on the table — and Badger doesn't mind a bit. He listens to their whole adventure and never once says «I told you so». Mole begins to like him very much.
fact: The Rat and the Mole wait «for what seemed a very long time, stamping in the snow to keep their feet warm».
fact: Mole says the footsteps sound like someone «walking in carpet slippers that were too large for him and down at heel» — «which was intelligent of Mole, because that was exactly what it was». (Down at heel = worn out and squashed at the back.)
fact: The Badger's first words are gruff: «Now, the very next time this happens, I shall be exceedingly angry.» (Exceedingly = very.)
fact: On hearing the Rat, he cries «What, Ratty, my dear little man!» «in quite a different voice», and says they «must be perished» (perished = frozen right through).
fact: He wears a long dressing-gown, carries a flat candlestick, and «had probably been on his way to bed». He pats both their heads and says «This is not the sort of night for small animals to be out.»
fact: He thinks the Rat has «been up to some of your pranks again» — though really it was the Mole who went into the Wild Wood alone.
fact: The passage is «long, gloomy, and, to tell the truth, decidedly shabby»; other tunnels branch off from a central hall, «mysterious and without apparent end».
fact: The kitchen has a red brick floor, a log fire between two chimney-corners, two high-backed settles (settle = a long wooden bench with a high back), a long table on trestles, and hams, herbs, onions and eggs hanging from the rafters.
fact: The book says it is a place «where heroes could fitly feast after victory, where weary harvesters could line up in scores along the table» (fitly = rightly) — and the plates «grinned at pots on the shelf», as if the room itself were smiling.
fact: Badger gives them dressing-gowns and slippers, and «bathed the Mole's shin with warm water and mended the cut with sticking-plaster».
fact: At supper «conversation was impossible for a long time»; then it was talk «with your mouth full», with elbows on the table and everybody speaking at once.
fact: Badger does not mind table manners, because he «did not go into Society» (Society = smart, polite company). The writer jokes that he was wrong, «because they do matter very much, though it would take too long to explain why».
fact: Badger «nodded gravely at intervals as the animals told their story», and did not seem surprised or shocked at anything.
fact: Badger «never said, “I told you so”» or that they ought to have done something else. «The Mole began to feel very friendly towards him.»
fact: Talk in this lesson: «bare» (London) = very, lots of; «dreich» (Scotland) = grey, cold and wet weather; «wee» (Scotland) = small; «it's giving» = it looks like, it feels like; «chuffed» (UK) = pleased; «bruv» (London) = brother, mate; «lowkey» = a bit, quietly.
exercise: How long do Rat and Mole wait at the door? | What seems a very long time, stamping in the snow | Only a second or two, because the Badger was sitting up waiting for them | About an hour, sitting quietly on the doormat they dug up | All night
exercise: What do the footsteps inside sound like, according to Mole? | Someone in carpet slippers too big for him | A heavy horse clattering slowly along a stone floor | A whole crowd of weasels running about inside | Nothing at all — the Badger walks quite silently
exercise: Was Mole right about the slippers? | Yes — that was exactly what it was | No — the Badger was barefoot and only pretended to shuffle | No, it was a hedgehog servant in clogs | Nobody ever finds out in the book
exercise: What shows first when the door opens a few inches? | A long snout and sleepy blinking eyes | A brass candlestick and a heavy wooden cudgel | A stripy paw holding a big iron key | A lantern held out by a little hedgehog
exercise: «Now, the very next time this happens, I shall be exceedingly angry.» What mood is the Badger in at first? | Gruff and suspicious, cross at being disturbed | Delighted and laughing, because he loves getting visitors at night | Frightened, because he thinks the weasels have come for him | Sleepy, but very polite and gentle with strangers
exercise: What changes the Badger's voice? | Hearing that it is Ratty, lost in the snow with his friend | Seeing that the Mole has brought him a present of honey | The bell ringing a second time, even louder | The sight of the Rat's two gleaming pistols
exercise: What does the Badger call the Rat? | «Ratty, my dear little man» | «You young rascal, out so late» | «Mr. Rat, sir, of the River» | «Water-boy»
exercise: «Why, you must be perished.» What does «perished» mean here? | Frozen right through | Dead | Very hungry and thirsty after a long walk | Lost for ever somewhere deep in the wood
exercise: What is the Badger wearing? | A long dressing-gown and old slippers | His best velvet suit, with a golden watch-chain | A thick coat and boots, ready to go out hunting | Goggles, a cap and an enormous overcoat
exercise: Where was the Badger probably going when they rang? | To bed | To his study, to read his books till morning | Out to hunt in the Wild Wood by moonlight | To the cellar
exercise: What does the Badger do to both animals, «paternally» (like a father)? | Pats their heads | Shakes their paws very hard and long | Tells them off for a whole hour | Sends them home
exercise: Whom does the Badger think has been up to «pranks»? | The Rat | The Mole, who went into the wood | Toad | The weasels and stoats
exercise: How does the book describe the passage to the kitchen? | Long, gloomy and decidedly shabby | Short, bright and freshly painted in green and gold | Covered in thick carpets and old pictures of badgers | Full of hedgehogs carrying candles
exercise: What is the floor of the kitchen made of? | Well-worn red brick | Polished oak boards, waxed every week | Earth and straw | Blue and white tiles from the old city
exercise: What hangs from the rafters? | Hams, herbs, onions and eggs | Lanterns, swords and old shields from the wars | Nothing at all — the ceiling is quite bare | Wet coats and boots, drying by the fire
exercise: What is a «settle» in Badger's kitchen? | A long wooden bench with a high back | A small round stool for one person only | A kind of hot sweet drink for winter nights | A long table standing on trestles
exercise: Who, says the book, could «fitly feast» in such a kitchen? | Heroes after victory, or weary harvesters | Only kings and queens with golden crowns | Toad and his rich friends from Toad Hall | Nobody — the book says it is far too small
exercise: What does the Badger do for the Mole's leg? | Bathes it and puts on sticking-plaster | Ties it up again with the Rat's handkerchief | Rubs butter into it and wraps it in a cabbage leaf | Nothing — he doesn't notice it at all
exercise: Why can't they talk at the start of supper? | They are too busy eating | The Badger strictly forbids any talking at the table | They are still too frightened to say a word | The Badger has fallen asleep in his chair
exercise: Which table manners does the Badger not mind? | Mouths full and elbows on the table | Throwing bread at each other across the benches | Feeding scraps to the dog under the table | Singing loudly between every mouthful
exercise: Why doesn't the Badger mind bad table manners? | He doesn't go into Society, so he thinks they don't matter | He has very bad manners himself and is proud of them | He is too sleepy to see what they are doing | He is far too polite to say anything
exercise: What does the Badger never say while they tell their story? | «I told you so» | «Well I never, what a night!» | «Come in» | «Supper's ready, sit down»
exercise: What does the Badger do while the animals tell their story? | Nods gravely, not shocked at anything | Laughs at them loudly, slapping the table with his paw | Writes it all down in a big book | Falls fast asleep in his arm-chair
exercise: How does the Mole feel about the Badger by the end of supper? | Very friendly towards him | Still rather frightened of him and his gruff voice | Bored with his long, slow stories | Angry that his house is so shabby
test: Jack: «Badger was well chuffed to get visitors that night, he loves company.» (chuffed = pleased) Put it right. | He grumbled at being disturbed, and only got friendly when he heard it was Ratty | Right: he had been waiting up, with the table already laid for two | He never let them in at all, and they slept in the snow | He was asleep, so the hedgehogs opened the door
test: Grandad, reading aloud: «down at heel, love — like my old slippers». What does «down at heel» mean? | Worn out and squashed down at the back | Very new and shiny, just out of the box | Much too tight for the feet | Lost under the bed
test: «It's bare cold out here, bruv, ring again.» (bare = very) Who rang the bell at the end of chapter III, and how? | The Mole swung on the bell-pull while the Rat hammered | The Rat rang it very politely, just once, and waited | The hedgehogs rang it from inside to let them know | Nobody rang — they knocked with their paws and shouted
test: The writer says it would «take too long to explain why» table manners matter. What is he doing? | Making a little joke, like a grown-up who can't quite explain | Giving a long, serious lesson on how to hold a knife and fork | Saying that table manners don't matter at all | Telling the Badger off with a list of rules
test: Mia from Glasgow: «Dreich night, they come in, and Badger gives them a right telling-off.» (dreich = cold, grey, wet) Put it right. | He never says «I told you so» | Right: he lectures them till midnight, then sends them off to bed hungry | He tells off only the Mole, for going into the wood alone | He shouts at the Rat for bringing the Mole
test: Is this TRUE or FALSE? «The Badger's kitchen is in a grand palace above ground.» | False: it is underground, down a long shabby passage | True: it is in a white palace right in the middle of the wood | True, and its windows look out over the river | False: it is up in a hollow tree
test: «Plates on the dresser grinned at pots on the shelf.» Can plates really grin? | No — the whole room just looks cheerful | Yes: in this book the plates are little animals too | Yes, because they have faces painted on them | No, it means the plates are cracked and chipped
test: Why does the kitchen feel so wonderful to Rat and Mole after this night? | They have just escaped the cold, frightening Wild Wood | It is the very first kitchen they have ever seen in their lives | It is full of all their friends from the river bank | It has a little river running through the middle of it
test: Looking back: in chapter I, how did the Badger behave at the picnic by the river? | Grunted «H'm! Company» and turned away | He came out and ate up all of the cold chicken and the ginger beer | He fell into the river and the Otter pulled him out | He sang them a long song about the Wild Wood
test: Looking back: in chapter III, the Rat told the Mole how to meet Badger. What did he say? | Take him not only as you find him, but when you find him | You must always write him a polite letter first and wait | You must bring him a present of honey every time | You must never, ever knock on his door
test: Looking back: how did the Mole cut his shin in chapter III? | On Badger's door-scraper, hidden in the snow | A weasel crept up and bit him in the dark | He cut it on the oar when he fell out of the boat | On a sharp branch, as he first thought
test: Looking back: what did the Rat take into the Wild Wood to find the Mole? | Two pistols and a stout cudgel | A lantern, a map and a long rope | Only the luncheon-basket, full of food | The Otter
test: Looking back: in chapter I the Rat said nobody interferes with Badger — «They'd better not.» What fits that warning now? | He is big and strong, the master of his house in the wood | He is tiny and shy, and hides from everyone in the wood | He has a hundred servants with swords and spears | He lives far away, beside the river
test: Ollie, lowkey bored: «it's giving cosy cabin, but it's just a kitchen.» (it's giving = it looks like) Why does the book spend so long on the kitchen? | To make us feel the warmth and safety after the terror outside | Because the Badger wants to sell his house and its kitchen | Because there is a big fight in the kitchen later on | It doesn't — the kitchen gets only one line
test: The Badger thinks the Rat «has been up to some of his pranks again». Is he fair? | Not really — it was the Mole who went in alone | Yes: the Rat planned the whole trip into the wood for fun | Yes, because the Rat broke his door with his stick | He is not talking about the Rat, but about Toad
test: What would happen if the Rat had not said his name at the door? | We can't be sure — but the gruff Badger might have stayed suspicious | He would have shot at them from the door | Nothing different — he lets in everyone | They would have frozen at once
test: ★★★ Count the animals at the supper table in this part (count the Badger too). | 3 | 2 | 4 | 5
test: ★★★ «Which was intelligent of Mole, because that was exactly what it was.» What is the joke? | Mole is praised like a detective — after being so slow with the door-mat | The writer really thinks the Mole is very stupid | Mole was wrong, and the writer is being kind | Mole could see right through the door
test: ★★★ The writer says table manners «do matter very much». Whom is he gently correcting? | The Badger | The Rat, for talking with his mouth full | The Mole | The reader, for reading books at dinner
exam: Identify the circumstance which occasioned the alteration in the Badger's manner of address. | His recognition of the Rat among the visitors | The ringing of the bell upon a second occasion with great force | The Mole's offer of payment for a night's lodging | The cessation of the snowfall
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Badger's conduct during the narration of the animals' misadventure? | He listened gravely, without surprise or reproach | He reproached them at length for their grave imprudence in entering the wood | He withdrew to his study and did not return | He laughed openly at their misfortune
exam: It is given that the Mole sustained an injury to the shin. Ascertain the treatment administered by the Badger. | Bathing with warm water and sticking-plaster | The binding of the limb with a handkerchief by the Rat alone | The summoning of a physician from the village | No treatment whatsoever
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Badger's view of table manners. | He held them unimportant, not frequenting Society | He insisted upon them rigorously at every meal without exception | He was entirely ignorant of their existence | He enforced them upon the hedgehogs alone
